# How the phx.new studio works

## The studio

The studio makes a Phoenix project with `mix phx.new`, compiles it, and runs it. It needs no Erlang, no Elixir, no Node.js and no server of yours. Try it:

* [In this browser](https://sntran.github.io/BEAM.com/phx/): the VM runs in the tab, as this Livebook does.
* [On Cloudflare Workers](https://phx.fifo.workers.dev): each visitor gets an instance, a Durable Object with its own VM, for 30 minutes.

The generator is the real one. The studio has the package `phx_new` of Hex in its release, and it calls `Mix.Tasks.Phx.New.run/1`, the function that `mix phx.new` runs in a terminal. The field `$ mix` of the studio runs the real generators of Phoenix and Ecto, such as `phx.gen.live`.

The studio does the other steps of `mix phx.new` in the VM, with no tools of your computer:

| On your computer | In the studio |
|---|---|
| `mix deps.get` gets the packages | The packages of a new project are in the release of the studio. |
| `mix compile` | Mix reads `mix.exs` and the config files. `Kernel.ParallelCompiler` compiles `lib/`. |
| `mix phx.server` | The app starts in the VM of the studio. One plug sends each request to the studio or to the app. |
| esbuild makes one file of `assets/js` | The browser loads the files as ES modules. The studio changes their imports. |
| The Tailwind CLI makes `app.css` | The compiler of Tailwind 4 runs in the page of the app. |

This notebook shows each step with code that runs in this Livebook, in the same kind of VM. The source of the studio is in [`examples/studio`](../../examples/studio).

## How a request goes

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  B[Browser] --> F[Studio.Front]
  F -- "/__studio/..." --> S[StudioWeb.Endpoint<br/>the studio, a LiveView]
  F -- "/assets/js/app.js<br/>/assets/css/app.css" --> A[Studio.Assets]
  F -- "any other path" --> E[HelloWeb.Endpoint<br/>the app of the project]
```

The app of the project runs at `/`, so its paths do not change. The studio is at `/__studio/`, and it shows the app in a frame.

## Compile in the VM

In a terminal, `mix compile` calls the compiler of Elixir. The studio calls it directly: `Kernel.ParallelCompiler.compile_to_path/3` compiles the files of `lib/`, loads the modules, and gives the warnings and the errors. The studio shows them in its tab Problems.

The next cell writes two files and compiles them. The second file has a warning.

```elixir
dir = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "studio_demo")
File.rm_rf!(dir)
File.mkdir_p!(Path.join(dir, "lib"))

File.write!(Path.join(dir, "lib/greeter.ex"), """
defmodule Demo.Greeter do
  def hello(name), do: "Hello, \#{name}, from \#{:erlang.system_info(:system_architecture)}"
end
""")

File.write!(Path.join(dir, "lib/unused.ex"), """
defmodule Demo.Unused do
  def one(x), do: 1
end
""")

files = Path.wildcard(Path.join(dir, "lib/**/*.ex"))

{us, {:ok, modules, %{compile_warnings: warnings}}} =
  :timer.tc(fn ->
    Kernel.ParallelCompiler.compile_to_path(files, Path.join(dir, "ebin"), return_diagnostics: true)
  end)

%{
  modules: modules,
  milliseconds: div(us, 1000),
  warnings: for(w <- warnings, do: {Path.basename(w.file), w.position, w.message})
}
```

The modules are in the VM now:

```elixir
Demo.Greeter.hello("Livebook")
```

A new project of `phx.new` has 12 modules. The studio compiles them in about 2 seconds in WebAssembly, and in about 1 second natively. After a save, it compiles all of `lib/` again and starts the app again.

## A Phoenix endpoint is a plug

`mix phx.server` starts an HTTP server for the endpoint. The studio does not do that: it starts the endpoint with `server: false`, and calls it as a plug. `Endpoint.call/2` runs the whole pipeline of the endpoint: its plugs, the router, and the controller.

The next cell makes a small app: a controller, a router and an endpoint. It starts the endpoint with no server, and sends it a request with `Plug.Test`.

```elixir
defmodule Demo.PageController do
  use Phoenix.Controller, formats: [:html]

  def home(conn, _params) do
    html(conn, "<h1>Hello from the app</h1><p>The path: #{conn.request_path}</p>")
  end
end

defmodule Demo.Router do
  use Phoenix.Router
  get "/", Demo.PageController, :home
  get "/about", Demo.PageController, :home
end

defmodule Demo.Endpoint do
  use Phoenix.Endpoint, otp_app: :demo
  plug Demo.Router
end

Application.put_env(:demo, Demo.Endpoint,
  server: false,
  secret_key_base: String.duplicate("s", 64)
)

case Demo.Endpoint.start_link() do
  {:ok, _pid} -> :started
  {:error, {:already_started, _pid}} -> :already_started
end

conn = Plug.Test.conn(:get, "/about") |> Demo.Endpoint.call(Demo.Endpoint.init([]))
{conn.status, conn.resp_body}
```

## One plug in front

The server of the studio has one plug, `Studio.Front`. It looks at the path, and it calls the studio, the asset steps, or the endpoint of the app. This is a small copy of it:

```elixir
defmodule Demo.Front do
  @behaviour Plug
  import Plug.Conn

  def init(opts), do: opts

  def call(%{path_info: ["__studio" | _]} = conn, _opts),
    do: send_resp(conn, 200, "the studio")

  def call(%{path_info: ["assets", "js", "app.js"]} = conn, _opts),
    do: send_resp(conn, 200, "the JavaScript step")

  def call(conn, _opts), do: Demo.Endpoint.call(conn, Demo.Endpoint.init([]))
end

for path <- ["/__studio/", "/assets/js/app.js", "/", "/about"] do
  conn = Plug.Test.conn(:get, path) |> Demo.Front.call([])
  {path, conn.status, conn.resp_body}
end
```

On Workers, an instance is at `/t/NAME/`, and the host removes that part of the path. The studio puts it back in `conn.script_name` and in the `url` of the endpoints, so each link that Phoenix makes starts with `/t/NAME`.

## JavaScript with no esbuild

A new project has `assets/js/app.js`. esbuild resolves its imports and makes one file. A browser can load ES modules, so the studio only changes each import to an address that the browser can load:

* a package of the release (`phoenix`, `phoenix_html`, `phoenix_live_view`) goes to its file in the release;
* a relative file (`../vendor/topbar`) goes to the file in `assets/`;
* `phoenix-colocated/APP` goes to the hooks that LiveView writes at the compile;
* another package goes to jsDelivr.

The LiveSocket of `phx.new` connects to `/live`. Under a path, such as `/t/NAME`, the studio puts the path before it. This is the function of the studio, with the start of the `app.js` of `phx.new`:

```elixir
defmodule Demo.Imports do
  @import ~r/(\bimport\s*(?:[\w*{}\s,$]+?\s*from\s*)?|\bexport\s*(?:[\w*{}\s,$]+?\s*)?from\s*|\bimport\s*\(\s*)(["'])([^"']+)\2/
  @packages ~w(phoenix phoenix_html phoenix_live_view)

  def transform(text, rel, base) do
    text = Regex.replace(@import, text, fn _, head, q, spec -> head <> q <> resolve(spec, rel, base) <> q end)
    Regex.replace(~r/new LiveSocket\(\s*(["'])\//, text, fn _, q -> "new LiveSocket(#{inspect(base)} + #{q}/" end)
  end

  defp resolve(spec, rel, base) do
    cond do
      spec in @packages ->
        base <> "/__studio/pkg/" <> spec <> ".js"

      String.starts_with?(spec, "phoenix-colocated/") ->
        base <> "/__studio/colocated/" <> String.replace_prefix(spec, "phoenix-colocated/", "") <> "/index.js"

      String.starts_with?(spec, ["./", "../"]) ->
        base <> "/__studio/js/" <> (rel |> Path.dirname() |> Path.join(spec) |> Path.expand("/") |> String.trim_leading("/"))

      true ->
        "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/" <> spec <> "/+esm"
    end
  end
end

app_js = ~S"""
import "phoenix_html"
import {Socket} from "phoenix"
import {LiveSocket} from "phoenix_live_view"
import {hooks as colocatedHooks} from "phoenix-colocated/hello"
import topbar from "../vendor/topbar"
import confetti from "canvas-confetti"

const liveSocket = new LiveSocket("/live", Socket, {hooks: {...colocatedHooks}})
"""

IO.puts(Demo.Imports.transform(app_js, "js/app.js", "/t/abc"))
```

A file with no `import` and no `export`, such as `topbar.js`, is a CommonJS or UMD file. The studio puts it in a module that gives `module.exports` as the default export.

## Tailwind in the browser

The Tailwind CLI reads `assets/css/app.css` and the files where the classes are, and it writes the CSS. Tailwind 4 has its compiler in JavaScript too, as the package `tailwindcss`. In the studio, the page of the app runs it:

1. The studio sends the text of `app.css` and the text of the files that the `@source` lines name.
2. The compiler of Tailwind reads `app.css`. The plugins of `phx.new` come from npm: daisyUI, and the heroicons that the page uses.
3. The page puts the CSS in a `<style>` element, and sends it to the studio. The studio serves it at `/assets/css/app.css` for the next load.

The next cell runs the same compiler in the frame of its output. It loads Tailwind from jsDelivr.

```elixir
defmodule Demo.Tailwind do
  use Kino.JS

  def new(html), do: Kino.JS.new(__MODULE__, html)

  asset "main.js" do
    """
    export async function init(ctx, html) {
      ctx.root.innerHTML = "<p>The compiler of Tailwind loads…</p>";
      const TW = "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/tailwindcss@4.3.3";
      const { compile } = await import(TW + "/+esm");
      const loadStylesheet = async (id, base) => {
        const path = TW + "/index.css";
        return { path, base: TW, content: await (await fetch(path)).text() };
      };
      const started = performance.now();
      const compiler = await compile('@import "tailwindcss";', { base: "/", loadStylesheet });
      const candidates = [...new Set(html.split(/[\\s"'`<>{}=;,]+/))];
      const css = compiler.build(candidates);
      const ms = Math.round(performance.now() - started);
      ctx.root.innerHTML = `<style>${css}</style>${html}` +
        `<p style="font: 13px monospace; margin-top: 12px">${css.length} bytes of CSS in ${ms} ms</p>`;
    }
    """
  end
end

Demo.Tailwind.new(~S"""
<div class="p-6 rounded-xl bg-orange-100 text-orange-950 shadow-md">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Styled by Tailwind 4</h2>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-orange-800">The compiler ran in the frame of this output.</p>
</div>
""")
```

## The generator and Mix

This Livebook has no Mix. The studio has `:mix` and `:phx_new` in its release, and it runs a task in a project as Mix does:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
Mix.shell(Mix.Shell.Process)

Mix.Project.in_project(:hello, "/tmp/studio/hello", fn _project ->
  Mix.Task.rerun("phx.gen.live", ~w(Blog Post posts title:string body:text))
end)
```

`Mix.Shell.Process` sends the output of the task to the process, as messages. The studio shows them in its tab Mix. Then it compiles the project again, and `Ecto.Migrator` of `phx.new` runs the new migration when the app starts.

## Two things that the studio found

The studio runs Ecto and a new Phoenix project in the WebAssembly runtime. Its tests found two faults of beam.com:

* The build for `--target wasm32` removed the attributes of each module. `Ecto.Repo` reads the behaviours of its adapter, so it refused `Ecto.Adapters.SQLite3`. The build now keeps the chunk `Attr`.
* SQLite in the VM used WAL mode for the files in memory, and the close of such a database did not end. `ecto_sqlite3` uses WAL by default, so the stop of a repo stopped the VM. SQLite of the runtime now has no WAL, as the files of the hosts already had.

See the [README of the studio](../../examples/studio/README.md) for its limits, and [WORKERS.md](../WORKERS.md) for the hosts.
